WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.510 --> 00:00:09.780
- Narrator: Every season of
"Game of Thrones" gave us a

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.200
new battle or two that
attempted to top the last.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.810
All the blood, gore, and
fireballs you saw on screen

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:18.810
would mean absolutely nothing without

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:20.870
the sound mix behind it.

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:23.870
From weapons, to wights, to music,

00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:26.310
a lot goes into what
you hear during a given

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:28.540
"Game of Thrones" action scene.

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:31.290
It's not just about how
they made all the sounds

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:33.969
but how many they need to make.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.073
Warning: Spoilers are coming.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:46.880
To break down how the sound
in the show's battle scenes

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:50.800
was done, we talked to Tim
Kimmel, the show's Emmy-winning

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.650
supervising sound
editor, who's been around

00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:54.950
since season three.

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:57.460
- I think one of the hardest
parts of these battle scenes

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:00.825
is when there's so much going
on, trying to find the detail

00:01:00.825 --> 00:01:04.400
to not make it just sound
like a wall of sound.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.170
- Narrator: That's no exaggeration.

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:09.480
The Battle of Winterfell
was especially complex.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.850
- I know we maxed out the
amount of tracks we were able to

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:13.240
use on the system we were using,

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:16.110
which I believe is 720 tracks.

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:19.519
And that was just for sound
effects, and sound design,

00:01:19.519 --> 00:01:23.580
and Foley, and backgrounds,
so I think on the dialogue side

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:26.700
there was probably another
200 tracks and another

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:28.970
60 tracks or so of music.

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:31.080
- Narrator: This is what
they were dealing with.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:34.340
It can take weeks to put
this much sound together.

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:36.761
Obviously, there's a lot going on here,

00:01:36.761 --> 00:01:41.178
so let's break down some
of these layers one by one.

00:01:46.275 --> 00:01:47.850
There are weapons of all shapes and

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:49.670
sizes on "Game of Thrones."

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:52.870
Weapons that catch fire,
kill White Walkers,

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:55.310
and bring dragons out of the sky.

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:57.870
Because the show has been on for so long,

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.340
the sound team had a
massive digital library of

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:03.368
prerecorded sounds to pull
from whenever necessary,

00:02:03.368 --> 00:02:05.520
but those sounds still had to be made

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:07.830
at one point in time.

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:10.663
Weapon sounds are usually made with Foley,

00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:13.160
a process in which real
objects are used to create the

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:16.555
sounds of humans and other
creatures interacting with the

00:02:16.555 --> 00:02:18.110
world around them.

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:19.020
So when you're hearing

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:22.410
two swords clashing against
each other, that was likely

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:25.830
the sound of two real
swords banging together.

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:28.090
The sound effects they
use will vary depending

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:29.860
on the character in the fight.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:32.960
For instance, one factor
that makes Jaime Lannister's

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:35.490
fights different from that
of the other characters:

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:36.890
his fake hand.

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:39.250
Kimmel recalls that the
sounds for Jaime's hand

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:42.510
were actually created using a frying pan.

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:46.260
Listen now, and you
might be able to hear it.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:51.224
- Euron: Ahhh!

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:55.020
There is also another
incredibly unique use

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:57.460
of Foley during the
Battle of the Bastards.

00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:59.660
- There's a moment
where all the arrows are

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:01.000
shot into the air.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:03.180
You hear nothing but the arrows
traveling through the air,

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.620
and instead of a very
typical whooshy sound of the

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:08.870
arrows flying by, we took all
of that out, and all Foley

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:10.590
did was the sound of the quills.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:13.420
You just hear that flapping
really lightly in the wind

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:16.317
before we then quickly dive
back down into the battle.

00:03:16.317 --> 00:03:17.173
- Loose!

00:03:29.878 --> 00:03:32.580
- The sounds of death are
such an important thing as

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.320
these battles get larger and larger as

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.080
the seasons moved along.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:38.020
- Narrator: A lot of characters
on this show have been

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:40.353
killed in a variety of ways.

00:03:43.176 --> 00:03:44.640
Chances are

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:48.130
you won't forget those
final screams and gurgles.

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:52.380
In these instances, top-notch
vocals are essential.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:56.700
After shooting is done, voice
actors will come into the

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:59.650
studio, partially to
provide all the background

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:02.046
noises you'll hear in
a given battle scene.

00:04:02.046 --> 00:04:05.200
These are known in
Hollywood as loop groups.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:07.023
The voice actors will give
many different options of the

00:04:07.023 --> 00:04:11.388
sound of, say, someone getting
stabbed or burned alive.

00:04:11.388 --> 00:04:13.370
Depending on the death,

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:16.440
sometimes it's a lower
grunt, and other times

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.930
it's a high-pitched screech.

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:22.092
In season eight, episode four,
"The Last of the Starks,"

00:04:22.092 --> 00:04:25.420
Rhaegal is brought down
by one of the scorpions.

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:28.480
Before editing and mixing,
the sounds were originally

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:31.810
made by sound designer Paula
Fairfield, who comes up

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:34.510
with most of the dragon
sounds on the show.

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:37.000
Getting the right sound for
this major death involves

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:38.820
some knowledge of anatomy.

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:41.760
- It started out with
a very screechy vocal,

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:45.450
painful vocal, and then
once it hits the throat,

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:47.160
if you listen closely, that vocal turns

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:48.360
into more of just of a gurgle.

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:51.470
You know, like it hit a vocal
chord, and it can't make much

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:53.930
sound, and it's sadly choking on

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:55.670
its own blood at that point.

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:58.000
Once it's about halfway
down towards the water,

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:02.000
you can see the vocals
just kind of disappear.

00:05:16.539 --> 00:05:18.330
- Narrator: Emmy-winning
composer Ramin Djawadi's

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:21.095
score has become an iconic
part of "Game of Thrones,"

00:05:21.095 --> 00:05:24.470
often providing the
show's emotional backbone.

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:26.990
While Djawadi and Kimmel work separately,

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:29.440
it's part of the sound
team's job to make sure all

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:32.360
elements of the sound work
together with the score.

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:34.460
- You know, that way we can
say, "Oh, look, he's going

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:37.030
really big in this moment.
Let's let music carry this,

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:39.650
and we'll provide some stuff
under it," or, "All right,

00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:41.270
he went quiet here.
Let's make sure we have

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:44.390
plenty of detail," or you know,
if he's using, say, higher

00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:47.890
strings in that moment,
frequency-wise, we know let's hit

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:50.740
the lower elements harder so
everything can work together.

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:52.760
- Narrator: During some of
the show's standout music

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:54.820
moments, you'll notice that other

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:57.487
sounds are much more toned-down.

00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:25.260
A major factor that drives
the sounds they make:

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:29.610
characters, and the ways
they do this are very subtle.

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:32.690
For instance, each army sounds different.

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:36.160
The Lannister army sounds
different from the Stark army,

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:38.430
which will both sound
different from the Dothraki

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:39.512
and the Unsullied.

00:06:39.512 --> 00:06:42.300
- Yeah, I mean, each army
had their own sounds, their

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:45.150
own kind of palette that we worked with

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:47.750
to kind of try to set them
apart from each other.

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:51.090
- Narrator: So a Lannister
spear might sound completely

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:54.590
different from a Stark spear,
and overall battles sound

00:06:54.590 --> 00:06:57.700
completely different from one another.

00:06:57.700 --> 00:07:00.320
The Battle of the Bastards
was a largely horse-driven

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:04.050
battle that then turned into
human-versus-human combat.

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:07.478
Meanwhile, battles like
Hardhome, Beyond the Wall,

00:07:07.478 --> 00:07:09.670
and Winterfell were about humans

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:12.300
versus wights and White Walkers.

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:15.130
Once you bring the undead
into a battle, you're dealing

00:07:15.130 --> 00:07:18.180
with many more layers of
different vocals and movements,

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:20.739
which often include the
sounds of bones to represent

00:07:20.739 --> 00:07:23.450
their often skinless bodies.

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:26.350
When balancing all this
sound, the most important part

00:07:26.350 --> 00:07:28.530
is not to lose track of
the moments bringing the

00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:30.760
characters and stories forward.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:33.010
- You know, make it feel like
a big battle, but make sure

00:07:33.010 --> 00:07:36.455
you hear that specific sword
of, you know, a main character

00:07:36.455 --> 00:07:37.990
who's attacking somebody.

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:41.350
So even though there's 30
people around him fighting,

00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:42.941
and you want to feel that,
you gotta make sure you can

00:07:42.941 --> 00:07:46.850
hear the detail of, you know,
your specific guy fighting,

00:07:46.850 --> 00:07:49.426
and his sword hitting whoever
he's fighting's sword,

00:07:49.426 --> 00:07:50.960
and if he stabs them, you know,

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:52.808
making all of this stuff cut through.

00:07:59.949 --> 00:08:03.618
- You know nothing, Jon Snow.

